ich British film producer co-produced a film starring Leonardo DeCaprio as a thief?", "answer": "Emma Thomas", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them is written about a topic in the Harry Potter universe, where is this topic derived from?", "answer": "derived from folklore, primarily Greek mythology, but also British and Scandinavian folklore", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Paul Thomas Anderson and Jon Turteltaub, are of which mutual nationality?", "answer": "American", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Do the genuses Luculia and Asystasia belong to the same family?", "answer": "no", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "What gang is rival to Islander 23 and was formed in 2012?", "answer": "Apex", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Edwin F. Davis, of Corning, Steuben County, New York was the first \"state electrician\" (executioner) for the State of New York, Davis performed 240 executions between 1890 and 1914, including the first person to be executed by electric chair, including which convicted murderer, and the first person in the world to be legally executed using an electric chair?", "answer": "William Francis Kemmler", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The River Ancholme in Lincolnshire in the east of England ends in which estuary?", "answer": "Humber Estuary", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which American director was raised in San Diego, CA, Dean Silvers or Juan Escobedo?", "answer": "Juan Escobedo", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Who was born first Buster Keaton or Pupi Avati?", "answer": "Joseph Frank \"Buster\" Keaton", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Skool Luv Affair is the second extended play by what seven member South Korean boy band that was formed by Big Hit Entertainment?", "answer": "BTS", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "which genus contains more species, Silene or Pimelea?", "answer": "Silene", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "The event known as \"The Show\", held at Adelaide's most popular event center, currently runs how long?", "answer": "10 days", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": " Zymer Bytyqi was the youngest to ever play in the Norwegian top league until it was broken by the player from which Dutch club?", "answer": "SC Heerenveen", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Alfred R. Kelman, is an American film and television documentary producer and director best known for his work on \"The Body Human\" and the 1984 television version of which 1984 British-American made-for-television film adaptation of Charles Dickens' famous 1843 novella of the same name?", "answer": "A Christmas Carol", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "rom March 2013 to October 2014, Omid Kordestani was the Non-executive Director at a company with headquarters in which city ?", "answer": "London", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Was Kristie Boogert or Alex O'Brien ranked higher as a singles player?", "answer": "No. 30 in singles", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Wall of Fame is a British comedy panel game that was first broadcast on Sky1 in 2011, it is hosted by \"Little Britain\" star David Walliams and features Tamara Ecclestone Rutland (born 28 June 1984) is a British model, socialite, television personality, and the daughter of which billionaire?", "answer": "Bernie Ecclestone", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What United States government agency did Philippe I. Reines, an American political consultant, join before founding Beacon Global Strategies in 2013 along with two other men?", "answer": "Department of State", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Archie Kemp, was an Australian boxer from Melbourne who died while fighting in which space in which a boxing match occurs, and in a modern ring, which is set on a raised platform, is square with a post at each corner to which four parallel rows of ropes are attached with a turnbuckle?", "answer": "boxing ring", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What country maufactures a magazine-fed, semi-automatic pistol which uses smart gun technology, used by a fictional character created by British journalist and novelist Ian Fleming in 1953?", "answer": "German company", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The podcast Pacific Coast Hellway is recorded in what entry-level luxury German car?", "answer": "BMW 3 Series", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "When was the king who was the subject of Siegfried Obermeier's \"The Secret Diaries of Ludwig II of Bavaria\" born? ", "answer": "25 August 1845", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Are Feist and Verona both singers?", "answer": "no", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Blue Vinyl and High School are both what?", "answer": "documentary film", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Approximately how many students are in the university in which Robert Kirthwood was the pioneer financial officer?", "answer": "2,800", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which genus of plant has more species, Melasphaerula or Weigela?", "answer": "Weigela is a genus of between six and 38 species", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "When was the singer of You Are My Destiny inducted into Canada's Walk of FAme", "answer": "2005", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Holme Moss is a moor in a region of England containing what forest in the west?", "answer": "Forest of Rossendale", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The only athlete to compete for Montserrat at the 2002 Commonwealth Games was born in what year?", "answer": "1971", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What was El Gallo de Oro's first album to become number one on the \"Billboard\" Top Latin Albums?", "answer": "Vencedor", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Reinhold Ewald and Klaus-Dietrich Flade were both German astronauts; however, which one visited the Mir space station aboard a Soyuz capsule in 1992?", "answer": "Klaus-Dietrich Flade", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Who is older, Julien Temple or Subhash Ghai?", "answer": "Subhash Ghai", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "The Civil Rights Heritage Center is housed in the former public swimming pool in what city?", "answer": "South Bend, Indiana", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which plant has more spikes projecting from its stems: Fouquieria or Alfalfa?", "answer": "Fouquieria", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Ian Penman, is a British writer, music journalist, and critic, he began his career as a writer for the \"NME\" in 1977, later contributing to various publications including Sight & Sound, a British monthly film magazine, published by which organization?", "answer": "British Film Institute (BFI)", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Steve Carell starred alongside Emma Stone in \"Battle of the Sexes\", which was loosely based on whom?", "answer": "King and Riggs", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What term describes the countries England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland and held the British Home Championship in 1959-60. ", "answer": "Home Nations", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The producer of this box office success starring Liam Neeson was born in what year?", "answer": "1971", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Trish Sie was a director for the upcoming musical comedy film written by whom?", "answer": "Kay Cannon", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who appointed the judge who wrote the lead opinion in Burnham v. Superior Court of California ?", "answer": "Ronald Reagan", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Love the Way You Lie was a song that was part of a medley with another song from what 2010 album?", "answer": "Loud", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which cartel holds one of Mexico's most-wanted drug lords and is fighting against the Zetas for control of Jalisco and its surrounding territories?", "answer": "Jalisco New Generation Cartel", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is another name for the NIR flag?", "answer": "Ulster Banner", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The American fashion designer for Bjork's Greatest Hits Tour is the director of what fashion house?", "answer": "Moschino", "type": "bridge"}, {"questi